<h2 id="list-heading">Top 8 Spiral Conveyor Manufacturers (A to Z)</h2>
<p>Each card links to the manufacturer's spiral product page. "Shortlist when" is our reading of the published specifications, not an endorsement.</p>
<MakerList items={makers} />

<h2 id="process-heading">Process Spirals Are a Separate Category</h2>
<p>Spirals that freeze, cool, or proof food on a long dwell-time belt are a different purchase from a spiral that lifts cases between floors. <a href="https://www.intralox.com/products/spirals" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intralox</a>, a Laitram division based in New Orleans, supplies the belting and drive technology for many of these. Its DirectDrive System engages the belt edge directly with the drum, and the company names proofers, coolers, freezers, and hydrochillers as applications. If your project is a process spiral, start with the belt and drive supplier and the spiral cage builder, not with the companies listed above.</p>

<h2 id="differences-heading">Four Design Differences to Compare</h2>
<h3>Where the drive sits</h3>
<p>Ryson pulls a slat chain from a single drive. Span Tech puts a sprocket on every tier and ties the shafts together mechanically. Multi-Conveyor uses a center drum. Nercon's washdown spiral and Intralox side-drive spirals drive the belt at its edge. Drive location sets how belt tension builds up through the tiers, how many motors the controls must handle, and where a technician has to reach for service.</p>
<h3>What the product rides on</h3>
<p>The surface can be slats, plastic top chain, table-top chain, or modular plastic belt. Slats and wide belts carry cases and totes. Narrow chains carry single-file containers and small packs. The surface also decides whether products can side-transfer on and off, and whether the spiral can be washed down.</p>
<h3>Size range</h3>
<p>Published sizes span a wide range: a 585 mm diameter compact spiral from FlexLink, a 1,400 mm four-tier helix from Dorner, 48 and 60 inch centerline spirals from Nercon, and Ryson spirals rated for 3,600 lbs on the spiral at one time. A supplier that fits a light primary-pack line may have nothing for full cases, and the reverse.</p>
<h3>Elevation or accumulation</h3>
<p>Some spirals are sold to change elevation in a small footprint. Others are sold to hold product for a set time, which makes them a buffer. Multi-Conveyor, AmbaFlex (AccuVeyor), and Dorner all describe accumulation uses. If you need buffer time, compare spirals against the other methods in the <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/buffering/">buffering system manufacturers</a> list.</p>

<SpecChecklist id="rfq-heading" title="Spiral Conveyor RFQ Checklist" items={[
  ['Product', 'Dimensions, weight, and packaging type for the smallest, largest, and typical item.'],
  ['Flow', 'Single file, side by side, or mass flow, and the rate in units per minute.'],
  ['Elevation', 'Infeed height, discharge height, and direction (up, down, or reversible).'],
  ['Footprint', 'Floor space available, ceiling height, and the infeed and discharge orientation.'],
  ['Entries and exits', 'Number of levels where product enters or leaves the spiral.'],
  ['Purpose', 'Elevation change only, or a stated buffer time in minutes.'],
  ['Loaded starts', 'Whether the spiral must stop and restart with product on every tier.'],
  ['Environment', 'Ambient, freezer, wet, or washdown, with the cleaning method and chemicals.'],
  ['Controls', 'Speed control, upstream and downstream handshakes, and the PLC platform.'],
  ['Service', 'Who installs, who commissions, and where spare chain and wear parts ship from.'],
]} />

